   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "GAMMA FROM INHIBITION. A patch of cortex at density, driven tonically, paced into the gamma band by its fast spiking cells: every pyramidal volley recruits the interneurons, their inhibition silences the patch for about one GABA-A decay, and the next volley follows (Whittington et al. 2000). The rate over time chart shows the rhythm, 34 Hz here.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": ""
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 230,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "TRY THIS: raise the drive and the band shifts up; lengthen the inhibitory time constant on the checkpoint (tau I, 6 ms) and the rhythm slows, since the silence after each volley lasts longer. Set the inhibitory weight to zero on the connect node and the rhythm goes with it.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "wiring"
   },
